1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,820
Divers pulled this
machine from a shipwreck.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:06,720
Then scientists realized
what it could calculate.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:12,480
In the spring of 1900, a boat of Greek
sponge divers was running from a storm.

4
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:14,880
They sheltered off Antikythera,

5
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:18,480
a small rocky island between
Crete and mainland Greece.

6
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:22,660
When the sea calmed, one diver
went down to look around.

7
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:26,640
According to the story the crew
told, he came back up terrified.

8
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:29,580
He said the seabed was covered in bodies.

9
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:32,200
Men and horses, lying in the dark.

10
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:35,780
His captain went down to see for himself.

11
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:37,660
The bodies were statues.

12
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:41,040
Bronze and marble, the cargo of
a ship that had sunk more than

13
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:44,960
two thousand years earlier,
probably on its way to Italy.

14
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:50,120
With help from the Greek navy, the
divers spent months bringing it up.

15
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:52,580
In canvas suits and copper helmets,

16
00:00:52,580 --> 00:00:56,660
at depths their bodies were never
built for, the work was brutal.

17
00:00:57,600 --> 00:00:58,980
One diver died.

18
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:00,780
Two were left paralyzed.

19
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:03,220
They raised statues, glass and pottery.

20
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:07,760
They also raised a shapeless lump
of corroded bronze and rotting wood.

21
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:10,240
Nobody paid it much attention.

22
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:13,720
Then, in 1902, the lump split apart.

23
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:18,640
An archaeologist named
Valerios Stais looked closer,

24
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:23,020
and saw something that did not seem
to belong in the ancient world.

25
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:25,120
A gear wheel.

26
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:26,480
And not just one.

27
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:31,980
Layers of gears, packed inside a
case about the size of a shoebox.

28
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:37,200
Some experts insisted it had to be a later
object that had fallen onto the wreck.

29
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:40,380
Nothing this intricate could be that old.

30
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:42,760
For decades it stayed a puzzle.

31
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:45,680
Then, in 2005, researchers scanned every

32
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:48,580
fragment with X-rays in
extraordinary detail.

33
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:52,040
They found about thirty surviving gears,

34
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:55,800
and thousands of tiny letters
hidden beneath the corrosion.

35
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:59,380
Slowly, its purpose became clear.

36
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:01,340
You turned a handle on the side.

37
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:04,760
And as the gears moved,
the machine moved the sky.

38
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:10,020
On the front, pointers carried the
Sun and the Moon through the zodiac,

39
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:14,040
around a calendar of three
hundred and sixty-five days.

40
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:19,640
A small turning ball, half dark and half
bright, showed the phase of the Moon.

41
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:24,180
On the back, spiral dials
counted long cycles of time.

42
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:27,460
One predicted eclipses, years in advance.

43
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:32,400
Another kept track of the great athletic
games of Greece, including the Olympics.

44
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,340
The most astonishing detail was the Moon.

45
00:02:36,360 --> 00:02:41,160
It does not cross the sky at a steady
speed, and Greek astronomers knew it.

46
00:02:41,460 --> 00:02:45,540
So the builder set two gears
on slightly offset axles,

47
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:48,220
joined by a pin sliding in a slot.

48
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:52,780
The Moon's pointer sped up and
slowed down, just like the real Moon.

49
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:56,680
It was probably built in
the second century BCE,

50
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:59,400
though the exact date is still debated.

51
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:05,020
Nothing of comparable complexity is known
again for more than a thousand years.

52
00:03:05,420 --> 00:03:08,020
So is this a machine that shouldn't exist?

53
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:09,460
Not quite.

54
00:03:09,910 --> 00:03:13,160
The Roman writer Cicero
described devices that showed

55
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:16,160
the motions of the Sun,
the Moon and the planets.

56
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:20,100
One, he wrote, was built by Archimedes.

57
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:23,260
For centuries, that
sounded like exaggeration.

58
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:26,580
The Antikythera mechanism
proves it was not.

59
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:28,680
Which leaves a harder question.

60
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:32,800
If the Greeks could build one,
they almost certainly built others.

61
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:39,060
But bronze was valuable, and old metal was
melted down and made into something new.

62
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:43,400
This one survived only because
it never reached the shore.

63
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:48,540
The same sea that swallowed the ship kept
the machine safe for two thousand years.

64
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:53,740
A single machine on the seabed held
a whole lost world of knowledge.

65
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:58,760
If you want to discover more forgotten
stories and lost inventions like this,

66
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:01,940
leave a like and subscribe
to Animated Tales.

67
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:04,260
Until next time.
